some 10 billion dollars of afghanistan's  money remains out of reach for the  taliban because the united states won't  allow it the white house has said the  reserves which are held in the u.s can't  be released due to legal reasons and  because the group is listed as a terror  organization  while that is true it is also true that  half of afghanistan's population is  facing hunger and people don't have  access to cash aid agencies like the  world food programme have repeatedly  asked for money to help afghans but the  question occupying many governments is  this  how do you help afghans without  inadvertently legitimizing or  financially helping the taliban  this hospital in the province of bamiyan  provides a stark reminder of the crisis  unfolding in afghanistan and also the  dilemma facing the international  community  more and more children are being  admitted here because they do not have  enough to eat  the un has warned that 55 percent of the  population are facing extreme levels of  hunger and that thousands could die this  winter  yet money that could be used to  alleviate the suffering there is frozen  in the united states  and there appears to be no chance of  that changing in the near future  the united states continues to face  difficult fundamental questions about  how it might be able to make reserve  funds available to directly benefit the  people of afghanistan with while  ensuring that the funds do not benefit  the taliban  when the taliban took power in august  washington blocked access to nearly 10  billion dollars in afghanistan's central  bank reserves held in the u.s  other institutions such as the imf also  stopped disbursing aid to the country  citing a lack of clarity about the  legitimacy of the new taliban-led  government in a rare interview the  afghan foreign minister pleaded with the  u.s to change its position  he said despite stripping women of their  rights and accusations that the taliban  summarily executes its opponents his  government can and should be trusted  making afghanistan unstable or having a  weak afghan government is not in the  interest of anyone it will not benefit  anyone  we are trying to have an afghanistan  which is not in conflict with anyone and  would stand on its own two feet in the  future  more humanitarian aid does appear to be  on its way and not just this offering  from china after the world bank said it  would transfer money directly to aid  organizations such as the world food  programme and unicef  but while aware of the urgent needs  facing afghanistan the u.s points out  that the taliban is a designated global  terrorist group and that this makes the  issue of releasing funds to them both  challenging  and complicated  and joining me now for more is  journalist ali latifi who has reported  on  afghanistan and from afghanistan for  years and now is in doha ali it's clear  afghans need help should the u.s release  the nearly 10 billion dollars in afghan  reserves it holds  well you know this what people keep  saying is that these reserves you know  this this money that it um  adds up to it belongs to the avalon  people so it's not really up to the  united states to decide what to do with  that you know yes it's being stored in  the federal reserve but  that doesn't mean that they should be  able to withhold it from the avalon  people who now more than ever really  really need it you know because they're  facing  a massive hunger crisis you know the the  value of the honey has dropped  precipitously  there's no employment there's no work  banks aren't able to function properly  so what a lot of people are saying is  that  you know it's not really up to yes it's  being housed in the united states but  it's not really necessarily up to the  united states who gets this money when  why or how i suppose the main question  that the us government and other  governments are facing at the moment is  how do you help afghans without  legitimizing or inadvertently  financially helping the taliban i mean  how does one do this  i mean  this is the issue that it's been five  months that they haven't been able to  answer this question yet you know they  haven't come up with a solution they  keep saying no no no no  or they keep adding new sort of um  demands or or restrictions to it you  know only if you meet these sorts of um  demands will we you know release the  funds but what they're not doing is in  the meantime figuring out a way to make  it work  you know it's been five months they  still haven't actually figured out some  kind of a mechanism to get money to  people that are in need uh without  quote-unquote legitimizing the taliban  and yet at the same time you know their  officials their leaders are coming to  kabul and meeting with them or are  meeting with them in doha so  you know to a lot of people in  afghanistan it's really strange because  it's like on the one hand you say you  don't want to legitimize them but then  on the other you're actually sitting  down with them and you're considering  reopening your embassies and you know  you are still sending some level of  money in so what is  you know what what is left to keep the  actual money that the avalon people need  back into the country to get it back in  let's take this a step forward and let's  look at it from the point of view of the  taliban i mean is there a case you think  for the taliban to be actually trusted  to help afghans get back on their feet  something that could potentially be in  the taliban's best interests and if this  indeed were the case then maybe a case  for  releasing money to afghanistan and by  extension the taliban  it's definitely in the taliban's best  interest because if they can pull it off  then they show look we were the ones to  save the avalon people right the former  governor government ran away  the us and the eu and all these other  countries you know they they cut off  their funding and then when they gave it  back to and in that amount of time  people were literally starving you know  people were selling their kidneys people  were selling their organs to make money  and now you know now that they've given  us the money again  we're able to distribute it and we're  able to keep people from being hungry  that's if they can pull it off now  that's a huge if  um because they haven't really shown  that you know they know how to govern  that they know how to provide services  that they know how to lead a government  they haven't shown any of those things  but the one thing that they will say is  that we're not corrupt like the former  government you know and that to them is  a big big selling point because  if you think about it at this point in  these last five six months there's  almost nobody that's saying oh if only  the former government came back came  back to power people were upset with  them people were fed up with the  corruption and the fraud and the lies  and everything that came from that  government  but  the taliban has not yet shown that they  can be trustworthy so it is a big test  for them and if they can pull it off  then yeah then they win because they say  look we're not as fraudulent as the  former government and when you gave us  that money we were actually able to  provide it to the people in need um  so now it's it's it's an issue of trust  on on the foreigner side and it's an  issue of the taliban actually being able  to carry out you know that that burden  that will be placed upon them to make  sure that it actually re reaches the  people that really really need it  it's not the last conversation we'll  have about this but we'll have to leave  it there for the time being ali latif in  doha thank you so much for joining us  today  thank you  you
 

